[center][size=6][b][color=#4fc3f7]7   D A S H E S   T O   D I E[/color][/b][/size]
[size=4][i]Rule 1 was cardio. Rule 2 is not standing still.[/i][/size]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/HannaPanda/7D2D-7DashesToDie/refs/heads/main/nexus/images/DashHero.jpg[/img][/center]

A horde rounds the corner. Normally that is the moment you find out how far the nearest doorway is. With this mod it is the moment you tap a key and are somewhere else.

[b]7 Dashes to Die[/b] adds a [b]dash[/b], an [b]air dash[/b] and a [b]double air dash[/b] - earned, not given. They live behind a new Agility perk that sits right next to Parkour: [b]Rule 2: Double Tap[/b].

[center][color=#4fc3f7]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/color][/center]

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]Still in development - and I want your numbers[/color][/b][/size]
This mod is [b]young and actively being worked on[/b]. It works, it is safe to play with, but expect changes between versions - and expect some of them to change how it [i]feels[/i], not just what it does. The default dash strength has already moved once: it started out carrying you 25 metres, which turned out to be about two and a half times too far.

[b]That is where you come in.[/b] Balance is the one thing I cannot test alone, because "right" depends on how you play. So if you shift the [b]Force[/b] slider, please drop a line in the [b]Posts[/b] tab:
[list]
[*]What percentage did you settle on?
[*]What made the default wrong for you - too short to escape, too far to aim, something else?
[*]Do you play with sprint a lot, or mostly standing dashes?
[/list]
A single number and one sentence is genuinely useful. If a value keeps coming up, it becomes the new default.

[quote]Settings you have changed yourself are [b]kept[/b] across updates. If you never touched a slider, an update can move it, because you are riding the default - which is exactly what happened to the dash strength in 1.0.4.[/quote]

[center][color=#4fc3f7]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/color][/center]

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]What it does[/color][/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Dash[/b] - a burst of speed in the direction you are moving. No input means straight ahead.
[*][b]Air dash[/b] - cancels your fall, so it carries you [i]flat[/i] across a gap instead of down into it.
[*][b]Double air dash[/b] - two dashes per jump at the last rank, recharging the moment you land.
[*][b]Costs stamina[/b] and has a cooldown. This is a tool, not a flight mode.
[/list]

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]It is a real perk, in the real skill tree[/color][/b][/size]
No console command, no free ability at level one. [b]Rule 2: Double Tap[/b] sits under [i]Agility ▸ Athletics[/i] and uses the same Agility requirements as every vanilla Agility perk.

[list=1]
[*][b]Rank 1[/b] (Agility 1) - unlocks the ground dash.
[*][b]Rank 2[/b] (Agility 3) - stronger dash, shorter cooldown.
[*][b]Rank 3[/b] (Agility 5) - [b]unlocks the air dash[/b].
[*][b]Rank 4[/b] (Agility 7) - stronger dash, shorter cooldown.
[*][b]Rank 5[/b] (Agility 10) - [b]unlocks the double air dash[/b].
[/list]

[quote]Not an Agility build this run? Turn [b]Require perk[/b] off in the settings and you get the full ability without spending a point.[/quote]

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]Your key, your binding - and your controller[/color][/b][/size]
The dash is registered with the game's own input system, so you rebind it exactly where you would expect: [i]Options ▸ Controls ▸ Movement[/i], in the [i]Player movement[/i] group, right below Jump and Crouch. Default is [b]V[/b].

[b]On a controller[/b] it has its own row under [i]Options ▸ Controller ▸ On Foot[/i], rebindable like any vanilla one. It ships [b]unbound[/b] on purpose: vanilla already uses every face button, bumper, trigger, stick click and d-pad direction bar one, and silently stealing the last free one would be a worse surprise than an empty row. Bind it to whatever you actually have spare.

[quote]This is new in [b]1.1.0[/b]. Earlier versions had a keyboard row only - the controller screen in 7DTD reads a completely separate list, which is not something you find out until someone with a gamepad tells you. Thank you to the player who did.[/quote]

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]Or just tap twice[/color][/b][/size]
The perk is called [i]Double Tap[/i], so as of 1.1.0 you can actually do that: [b]tap a movement key twice and you dash that way[/b]. Double-tap A and you dodge left, even if you are holding W at the time.

It is [b]off by default[/b], because a mis-read tap costs you stamina and moves you somewhere you did not ask to go - that should be a choice, not a surprise. Turn it on in the settings, and tune the window if it does not fit your hands:
[list]
[*][b]300 ms[/b] (the default) - reachable without aiming for it, still tight enough that normal walking does not trip it.
[*][b]Lower it toward 150[/b] if dashes start firing on their own while you strafe.
[*][b]Raise it toward 600[/b] if you keep trying and nothing happens.
[/list]
The dash key still works exactly as before. This is a second way in, not a replacement - and a double tap costs the same stamina and obeys the same cooldown, so it is not the cheap option either.

[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/HannaPanda/7D2D-7DashesToDie/refs/heads/main/nexus/images/DashKey.jpg[/img][/center]

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]Configurable in game[/color][/b][/size]
With [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/7daystodie/mods/4017][b]Gears[/b][/url] installed you get a settings page under [i]Mods ▸ 7 Dashes to Die[/i], reachable from the main menu or the ESC menu:
[list]
[*][b]Enabled[/b] - on or off.
[*][b]Require perk[/b] - off gives you the full ability without spending skill points.
[*][b]Force[/b] - 25 to 300%. How far a dash carries you: roughly 10 m at the default, about 6 m at the bottom of the range and 75 m at the top. [i]If you change this one, please tell me what you picked.[/i]
[*][b]Cooldown[/b] - 0.2 to 10 seconds. Higher perk ranks shorten it further.
[*][b]Stamina cost[/b] - 0 to 50. Set it to 0 for a free dash.
[*][b]Volume[/b] - 0 to 100%, on top of your normal game volume.
[*][b]Double tap to dash[/b] - off by default. Tap a movement key twice to dash that way.
[*][b]Double tap window[/b] - 150 to 600 ms, default 300. How close together the two taps have to be.
[/list]
Settings are read on every dash, so a slider you move now takes effect on the next one. [b]No restart, no new save.[/b]

[quote]Gears is [b]optional[/b]. Without it the mod runs on its built-in defaults. Nothing breaks, nothing errors, you just do not get the menu.[/quote]

[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/HannaPanda/7D2D-7DashesToDie/refs/heads/main/nexus/images/DashOptions.jpg[/img][/center]

[center][color=#4fc3f7]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/color][/center]

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]Requirements[/color][/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Tested on V 3.0.0, V 3.0.1 and V 3.1.0.[/b] Each one was launched with the mod installed and the log checked - no errors, no exceptions - and then actually played: the controller row binds and survives a controller reset, and the double tap fires. Each was also started with a binding already saved, and restored it. The dash itself was play-tested and tuned on [b]V 3.0.1 (b4)[/b]. Other 3.x builds are [i]untested[/i], not known-broken - the whole ability is a Harmony DLL, so every release has to re-earn that list. If you run it on another build, I would like to hear how it went.
[*][b]EasyAntiCheat must be off.[/b] This mod ships a Harmony DLL. Single-player and private servers.
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/7daystodie/mods/4017][b]Gears[/b][/url] - [b]optional[/b], only for the settings menu. Gears itself needs Quartz.
[*][b]Multiplayer:[/b] install it on the client for the ability, and on the server as well so the perk exists in the skill tree.
[/list]

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]Installation[/color][/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Install the zip with Vortex or MO2, or extract the [i]SevenDashesToDie[/i] folder into [i]7 Days To Die/Mods/[/i].
[*]Launch with EAC disabled.
[*]Spend a point on [i]Agility ▸ Athletics ▸ Rule 2: Double Tap[/i].
[/list]

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]Adding and removing mid-save[/color][/b][/size]
Adding it mid-save is fine. [b]Removing it is safe too[/b], with one caveat worth knowing: the perk disappears from the skill tree, and any points you spent on it are gone with it. That is how 7 Days to Die handles a removed perk, not something this mod can prevent. Nothing else is touched - no blocks, no items, no recipes, no entities.

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]Performance[/color][/b][/size]
One Harmony postfix on the local player's update, which reads a handful of key states and returns. The dash itself hands a single impulse to the movement controller the game already runs. No new objects, no extra physics. The double-tap tracker is four booleans and four timestamps, and it does not touch the settings until a second tap actually lands.

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]Honest notes[/color][/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]There is no dash animation.[/b] The game has no such move in its rig, and a mod cannot add one. In first person you will not notice; other players see you glide rather than plant a foot.
[*][b]Multiplayer replication is positional.[/b] 7 Days to Die sends positions, not impulses, so on a laggy connection your dash looks snappier to you than to everyone else.
[*][b]Localized into 13 languages[/b] - EN, DE, ES, FR, IT, JA, KO, PL, PT-BR, RU, TR, ZH-Hans, ZH-Hant.
[/list]

[center][color=#4fc3f7]━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━[/color][/center]

[size=5][b][color=#4fc3f7]Changelog[/color][/b][/size]
[size=3][b]1.1.1[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Fixed: your dash binding survives a restart.[/b] The game applies saved bindings before any mod has loaded, so the dash was falling back to its default on every launch. Reported for the controller button, but the keyboard key had exactly the same hole - it just looked healthy if you had left it on V. Nothing was ever lost on your side: the binding sat on disk the whole time and simply never came back. If you gave up on rebinding it, it is worth another go.
[/list]
[size=3][b]1.1.0[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]New: the dash is bindable to a controller button[/b] - its own row under [i]Options ▸ Controller ▸ On Foot[/i], and it stays there after "Reset to defaults". Ships unbound, because vanilla already uses every button worth stealing.
[*][b]New: optional double tap.[/b] Tap a movement key twice and you dash that way. Off by default; the window is adjustable from 150 to 600 ms.
[*]The ability itself is untouched - same force, same cooldown, same ranks. Your key binding and your settings carry over.
[/list]
[size=3][b]1.0.4[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]The dash is about a quarter as strong[/b], and that number is measured rather than guessed. It used to carry you 25 metres from a standstill - a whole street. Now it is roughly 10. [b]If you liked the old one, set Force to 250%.[/b]
[*]Sprint dashes got slightly shorter as a side effect, which is the range the momentum rule was designed for.
[/list]
[size=3][b]1.0.3[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]A sprint dash no longer inherits your full sprint speed.[/b] Only the part of it already going the way you dash counts, and only a quarter of that - so dashing forward out of a sprint is still the fastest thing you can do, while dashing sideways is not a free rocket. A backdash keeps full strength; it is the panic button.
[/list]
[size=3][b]1.0.2[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Fixed: dashing always went straight ahead[/b], whichever way you were actually moving. Sideways and backwards dashes work now.
[*]The dash has its own sound instead of borrowing the game's swoosh.
[*]It respects inverted controls and refuses to fire while your movement is disabled.
[/list]
[size=3][b]1.0.1[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Fixed: the dash key did not appear in Options ▸ Controls[/b], so there was no way to see or rebind it.
[/list]
[size=3][b]1.0.0[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]First release for 7 Days to Die V3.0.
[/list]
[i]Full history: [url=https://github.com/HannaPanda/7D2D-7DashesToDie/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md]CHANGELOG.md[/url] on GitHub.[/i]

